<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
Full body oxygen delivery is calculated by multiplying the oxygen content of the blood by the cardiac output. This process ensures that the body receives the necessary oxygen to sustain its metabolic functions. The calculation is:  
\[
\text{Full body oxygen delivery} = \text{Cardiac output} \times \text{Oxygen content of blood}
\]  
The oxygen content of the blood is calculated as:  
\[
\text{Oxygen content of blood} = \text{Oxygen content in arterial blood} \times \text{Blood volume}
\]  
The oxygen content in arterial blood is calculated as:  
\[
\text{Oxygen content in arterial blood} = \text{Oxygen content in arterial blood} = \text{Oxygen content in plasma} + \text{Oxygen content in red blood cells}
\]  
The oxygen content in plasma is calculated as:  
\[
\text{Oxygen content in plasma} = \text{Oxygen partial pressure in arterial blood} \times \text{Plasma volume}
\]  
The oxygen content in red blood cells is calculated as:  
\[
\text{Oxygen content in red blood cells} = \text{Oxygen content in arterial blood} = \text{Oxygen partial pressure in arterial blood} \times \text{Red blood cell volume}
\]  

This comprehensive approach ensures accurate calculation of full body oxygen delivery, which is critical for maintaining physiological balance.
