","To calculate the change in entropy (ΔS) for the reaction:  
\[
3 \text{CO}(g) + \frac{3}{2} \text{O}_2(g) \rightarrow 3 \text{CO}_2(g)
\]  
at constant pressure and 298 K, we use the Gibbs free energy equation:  
\[
\Delta S = \sum S_{\text{rxn}} = \sum S_{\text{products}} - \sum S_{\text{products}}
\]  
For gas-phase reactions, the entropy of a substance is given by the Gibbs equation:  
\[
S = \frac{R}{T} \ln \frac{P}{R_0}
\]  
However, since the reaction is carried out at constant pressure, the change in entropy can be calculated using the standard change in entropy:  
\[
\Delta S = \Delta S_{\text{rxn}} = \sum n_{\text{rxn}} \Delta S_0
\]  
where \( n_{\text{rxn}} \) is the stoichiometric coefficient of the substance in the reaction, and \( \Delta S_0 \) is the standard entropy change for the substance.  
From the given reaction, we have:  
\[
3 \text{CO}(g) + \frac{3}{2} \text{O}_2(g] \rightarrow 3 \text{CO}_2(g)
\]  
From the standard heat capacity tables, we can find the standard entropy values for CO, O₂, and CO₂ at 298 K:  
\[
S_0(\text{CO}) = 100.0 \text{ J/(mol·K})
\]  
\[
S_0(\text{O}_2) = 241.0 \text{ J/(mol·K})
\]  
\[
S_0(\text{CO}_2) = 230.0 \text{ J/(mol·K})
\]  
Substituting these values into the equation for ΔS:  
\[
\Delta S = 3 \times S_0(\text{CO}_2) - 3 \times S_0(\text{CO}) - \frac{3}{2} \times S_0(\text{O}_2)
\]  
\[
\Delta S = 3 \times 230.0 - 3 \times 100.0 - \frac{3}{2} \times 241.text{ J/(mol·K]}
\]  
\[
\Delta S = 690 - 300 - 361.5 = 28.5 \text{ J/K}
\]  
Thus, the change in entropy for the reaction is \(\boxed{28.5 \text{ J/K}}\).",relevance,"# Task Description
