   - NOE experiments involve the detection of coupling between nuclei in close spatial proximity. By analyzing the intensity of the NOE peaks, the relative positions of the protons in the chiral alcohol can be determined.  
   - For example, if the alcohol oxygen proton (δ) is close to the proton at C-1 (the carbon where the Grignard addition occurs), the NOE peaks for δ and C-1 protons will be strong. This information helps assign the relative positions of the protons and predict the R/S configuration.  
   - 2D-COSY experiments can also help identify neighboring protons, aiding in the assignment of the R/S configuration.  

2. **Infrared (IR) Spectroscopy:**  
   - The IR spectrum of the alcohol will show characteristic peaks for O-H stretching (10–15 eV) and C-O stretching (8–10 eV).  
   - The exact positions of these peaks can be influenced by the spatial arrangement of the molecule, providing additional insights into the structure.  

3. **Mass Spectrometry (MS):**  
   - Mass spectrometry can help confirm the molecular weight and fragmentation pattern of the alcohol, supporting its identity.  

4. **Nuclear Magnetic Resonance (NMR) Spectroscopy:**  
   - The 13C NMR spectrum will show the carbon atoms in the alcohol, including the chiral carbon.  
   - The 1H NMR spectrum will show the protons in the alcohol. Coupling patterns and chemical shifts can provide information about the spatial arrangement of the protons, which can be used to predict the R/S configuration.  

