","To calculate the Gibbs free energy change (\(\Delta G\)) for the electrochemical reaction, we use the Nernst equation:

\[
\Delta G = -n F \Delta E
\]

where:
- \(n\) is the number of electrons transferred in the reaction.
- \(F\) is the Faraday constant (96,000 C/mol).
- \(\Delta E\) is the standard cell potential (\(\Delta E\)).

First, calculate the standard cell potential (\(\Delta E\)) using the standard standard reduction potentials:

\[
\Delta E = E_{oxidant} - E_{reductant}
\]

For the given reaction:
\[
Zn(s) + Cu^{2+}(aq) \rightarrow Zn^{2+}(aq) + Cu(s)
\]

- The cathode is the reduction half-cell: \(Cu^{2+}(aq) + 2e^- \rightarrow Cu(s)\).  
  The standard standard reduction potential is \(E_{Cu} = +0.34 \, \text{V}\).
- The anode is the oxidation half-cell: \(Zn(s) \rightarrow Zn^{2+}(aq) + 2e^-\).  
  The standard standard reduction potential is \(E_Z = -0.76 \, \text{V}\).

Since the reaction is oxidation at the anode, the standard potential for the oxidation half-cell is the negative of the standard reduction potential: \(E_{Zn} = -(-0.76 \, \text{V}) = 0.76 \, \text{V}\).

Thus, the standard cell potential is:
\[
\Delta E = E_{Cu} - E_{Zn} = 0.34 \, \text{V} - (-0.76 \, \text{V}) = 0.34 \, \text{V} + 0.76 \, \text{V} = 1.10 \, \text{V}
\]

Next, determine the number of electrons transferred (\(n\)).  
The reaction involves the transfer of 2 electrons per mole of \(Zn\) or \(Cu\).  
Thus, \(n = 2\).

Finally, calculate the Gibbs free energy change (\(\Delta G\)):
\[
\Delta G = -n F \Delta E = -2 \times 96,000 \, \text{C/mol} \times 1.10 \, \text{V} = -201,600 \, \text{J/mol}
\]

Convert to kJ/mol:
