The octahedral coordination geometry allows for the reversible binding and release of oxygen molecules. When hemoglobin encounters an oxygen-rich environment (such as in the lungs), the oxygen molecules bind to the iron ions, forming oxyhemoglobin. In oxygen-poor environments (such as in tissues), the oxygen molecules are released, and the hemoglobin returns to its deoxyhemoglobin state.

