In contrast, other types of asymmetric synthesis may favor one enantiomer (enantioselectivity) but do not explicitly control the absolute configuration. The product may be predominantly one enantiomer, but the reaction conditions or catalyst may not provide information about which enantiomer is formed.  

For example, if a chiral catalyst gives a mixture of 90% R and 10% S, the reaction is enantioselective but not absolutely asymmetric. The product is predominantly R, but the reaction does not specify whether the R is the correct R or the opposite R.  

