","To determine the maximum percentage change in the wavelength of a 2.00 Å photon scattered by an electron, we use the Compton scattering formula:

\[
\lambda_{\text{scatt}} = \lambda_{\text{scatt}} + \frac{2\lambda_{\text{scatt}}}{m_{\text{e}}} \cos \theta
\]

where:
- \(\lambda_{\text{scatt}}\) is the wavelength of the scattered photon.
- \(\lambda_{\text{scatt}}\) is the wavelength of the incident photon.
- \(m_{\text{e}}\) is the mass of the electron (9.34 \times 10^{-3} \text{ kg}).
- \(\theta\) is the angle of scattering.

The maximum change in wavelength occurs when the scattering angle \(\theta = 0\) (head-on collision), where \(\cos \theta = 1\).

Substituting \(\cos \theta = 1\) into the Compton scattering formula:

\[
\Delta \lambda = \lambda_{\text{scatt}} - \lambda_{\text{inc}} = \frac{2\lambda_{\text{inc}}}{m_{\text{e}}}
\]

Substituting the given values:
\[
\Delta \lambda = \frac{2 \times 2.00 \times 10^{-10} \text{ Å}}{9.34 \times 10^{-3} \text{ kg}}
\]

First, convert the wavelength to meters:
\[
\Delta \lambda = \frac{2 \times 2.00 \times 10^{-10} \times 10^{-3} \text{ m}}{9.34 \times 10^{-3} \text{ kg}} = \frac{4.00 \times 10^{-13} \text{ m}}{9.34 \times 10^{-3} \text{ kg}} \approx 4.18 \times 10^{-11} \text{ m}
\]

Now, convert this change in wavelength back to Ångströms:
\[
\Delta \lambda = 4.18 \times 10^{-11} \text{ m} \times \frac{10^{-3} \text{ m}}{1 \text{ Å}) = 4.18 \times 10^{-14} \text{ Å}
\]

The percentage change in wavelength is given by:
\[
\Delta p = \frac{\Delta \lambda}{\lambda_{\text{inc}}} \times 100\% = \frac{4.18 \times 10^{-14} \text{ Å}}{2.00 \times 10^{-10} \text{ Å}} \times 100\% = 0.00209 \times 100\% = 0.209\%
\]

Thus, the maximum percentage change in the wavelength of a 2.00 Å photon scattered by an electron is approximately \(\boxed{0.21\%}\).

