To calculate the work done, we use the formula:  
\[
W = F \cdot d \cdot \cos(\theta)
\]  
where \( W \) is the work done, \( F \) is the force applied, \( d \) is the displacement, and \( \theta \) is the angle between the force and the displacement vectors.  
Given:  
- \( F = 60.0 \, \text{N} \).  
- \( \theta = 60.0^\circ \).  
- \( d = 10.0 \, \text{m} \).  
Substituting the values:  
\[
W = 60.0 \, \text{N} \times 10.0 \, \text{m} \times \cos(60.0^\circ)
\]  
Since \(\cos(60.0^\circ) = 0.5\), the work is:  
\[
W = 600 \, \text{N m} \times 0.5 = 300 \, \text{J}
\]  
Thus, the work done is \(\boxed{300 \, \text{J}}\).
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

