","To show that the Dufort-Frankel method is stable for any \(\Delta t\), we consider the diffusion equation \(\frac{\partial \phi}{\partial t} = \kappa \frac{\partial^2 \phi}{\partial x^2}\). The Dufort-Frankel method is given by:
\[
\phi_{j}^{n+1} = \phi_j^{n-1} + \frac{2\kappa \Delta t}{(\Delta x)^2} \left( \phi_{j+1}^n - \phi_j^{n+1} - \phi_j^{n-1} + \phi_{j-1}^n \right].
\]
Rearranging the equation, we get:
\[
\phi_{j}^{n+1} + \frac{\kappa \Delta t}{(\Delta x)^2} \phi_{j}^{n+1} = \phi_j^{n-1} + \frac{2\kappa \Delta t}{(\Delta x)^2} \left ( \phi_{j+1}^n - \phi_j^{n-1} + \phi_{j-1}^n \right).
\]
\[
\left( 1 + \frac{\2\kappa \Delta t}{(\Delta x)^2} \right) \phi_{j}^{n+1} = \phi_j^{n-1} + \frac{2\kappa \Delta t}{(\Delta x)^2} \left \phi_{j+1}^n - \phi_{j}^n + \phi_{j-1}^n \right).
\]
Rearranging the equation, we get:
\[
\left( 1 + \frac{\kappa \Delta t}{(\Delta x)^2} \right) \phi_{j}^{n+1} = \frac{\kappa \Delta t}{(\Delta x)^2} \phi_{j-1}^n + \left ( 1 - \frac{\kappa \Delta t}{(\Delta x)^2} \right ) \phi_j^n + \frac{\kappa \Delta t}{(\Delta x)^2} \phi_{j+1}^n.
\]
Dividing by \(\Delta t\), we get:
\[
\frac{1}{\Delta t} + \frac{\kappa}{\Delta x^2} \phi_{j}^{n+1} = \frac{\kappa}{\Delta x^2} \phi_{j-1}^n + \frac{1 - \kappa \Delta t/\Delta x^2}{\Delta t} \phi_j^n + \frac{\kappa}{\Delta x^2} \phi_{j+1}^n.
\]
This can be written as:
\[
\phi_{j}^{(n+1)} = \frac{1}{\Delta t} \phi_j^n + \frac{\kappa \Delta t}{\Delta x^2} \left \phi_{j-1}^n + \frac{\kappa \Delta t}{\Delta x^2} \phi_{j+1}^n - \frac{\kappa \Delta t}{\Delta x^2} \phi_{j}^n.
\]
The general form of the discrete diffusion equation is:
\[
 \phi_{j+1} = \frac{\kappa \Delta t}{\Delta x^2} \phi_{j-1} + \left 1 - \frac{\kappa \Delta t}{\Delta x^2} \right \phi_j + \frac{\kappa \Delta t}{\Delta x^2} \phi_{j+1}.
