","To calculate the change in Gibbs free energy for the reaction, we use the standard Gibbs free energy change for the reaction:  
\[
\Delta G = \sum n \Delta G_0(products) - \sum n \Delta G_0(products)
\]  
where \( n \) is the stoichiometric coefficient of the substance and \( \Delta G_0 \) is the standard Gibbs free energy of formation.  
The reaction is:  
\[
2 \text{H}_2 + \text{O}_2 \rightarrow 2 \text{H}_2\text{O}
\]  
Given:  
- Standard Gibbs free energy of formation of water (\(\Delta G_0(H_2\text{O})\)) = -237.1 kJ/mol  
- Standard Gibbs free energy of formation of hydrogen (\(\Delta G_0(\text{H}_2}\)) = 0 kJ/mol (since it is a gas in its standard state)  
- Standard Gibbs free energy of formation of oxygen (\(\Delta G_0(\text{O}_2}\)) = 0 kJ/mol (since it is a gas in its standard state)  
Substituting the values:  
\[
\Delta G = 2 \times \Delta G_0(\text{H}_2\text{O}) - 2 \times \Delta G_0(\text{H}_2) - 1 \times \Delta G_0(\text{O}_2)
\]  
\[
\Delta G = 2 \times (-237.1 \text{ kJ/mol}) - 2 \times 0 \text{ kJ/mol} - 1 \times 0 \text{ kJ/mol}
\]  
\[
\Delta G = -474.2 \text{ kJ/mol}
\]  
Since the reaction involves 2 moles of hydrogen gas and 1 mole of oxygen gas, the change in Gibbs free energy for the given amounts is:  
\[
\Delta G = 2 \times (-474.2 \text{ kJ/mol}) = -948.4 \text{ kJ}
\]  
Thus, the change in Gibbs free energy is \(\boxed{-474.2 \text{ kJ}}\).",fluency,"# Task Description
