   \[
   V_2 = - \frac{\mu N_1 N_2 A}{2\pi \ell}^2 \frac{dI_1(t)}{dt}
   \]

4. **Given \( I_1(t) \) and \( V_0 \)**: The source is given as \( V_0 \), which is the r.m.s. voltage. The alternating current is given by \( I_1(t) = I_{1\text{rms}} \sqrt{2} \cos(\omega t) \), where \( I_{1\text{rms}} \) is the r. m.s. current and \( \omega \) is the angular frequency.
